Daily flow (once set)

Work → git add -A → git commit -m "checkpoint: …" → git push (sends dev to GitHub).


____________________________



This saves a branch point to start here when starting new thread. 

git add -A → stage all current changes (pick what goes in the checkpoint).

git commit -m "message" → create the checkpoint (snapshot) on your current branch (dev).

git tag v2025.10.20-menu-clean → label that checkpoint so it’s easy to jump back to later (optional).

Does this become the “starting point” for dev?

The latest commit on dev is always your new starting point.

A tag is just a named pointer to that commit; it doesn’t change the branch.

Minimal flow (do this)
git add -A
git commit -m "checkpoint: menu cleanup; tests pending"      ## my comments in the ""
git tag v2025.10.20-menu-clean      ## don't have to do this one but can try it. 
# (optional publish)
git push && git push origin v2025.10.20-menu-clean  ## only if your online. 

Check
git log --oneline 




